    "result_text": "The full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, all tests passing).\n\n## Summary\n\nI verified the requirement is fully satisfied. All rejection responses (400, 409, 429) already return an RFC7807 `application/problem+json` body with the four required members.\n\nThe mechanism lives in `ExceptionControllerAdvice.java`:\n\n- **`detailBuild(...)`** (lines 65\u201373) constructs a Spring `ProblemDetail` and populates every required member:\n  - `type` \u2014 `problemDetail.setType(...)` (the request URL, a valid URI)\n  - `title` \u2014 `problemDetail.setTitle(e.getClass().getSimpleName())`\n  - `status` \u2014 carried by `ProblemDetail.forStatus(status)`\n  - `detail` \u2014 `problemDetail.setDetail(detail)`\n- Every rejection handler routes through `detailBuild` and returns `ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>`:\n  - **400** \u2014 `MethodArgumentNotValidException`, `MissingOwnerFieldsException`, `InvalidTelephone/Email/Postcode`, `DisposableEmail`, `FutureRegistrationDate`\n  - **409** \u2014 `DuplicateIdentity`, `HouseholdDuplicate`, `CityAtCapacity`\n  - **429** \u2014 `DailyOwnerLimit`\n\nUnder Spring Framework 7 / Boot 4.1, returning a `ProblemDetail` body causes the framework to serialize it and set the `application/problem+json` content type automatically. I confirmed no controller bypasses the advice with a hand-rolled error body, and the HTTP status codes are unchanged.\n\nThe new `AcceptanceTest.coreRejectionIsProblemJson` (asserting the `application/problem+json` content type and `$.status == 400` on a missing-`city` create) passes, along with the rest of the suite.",
